Id: lin045i
Severity: INFO
Category: kernel
Check: check_sysctl
Fix: Set fs.protected_fifos=2 in /etc/sysctl.d.
References: Linux kernel sysctl documentation (admin-guide/sysctl/fs, protected_fifos); sysctl.d(5)
Controls: CIS 4.1, 10.5; NIST CM-6, SI-16; ISO 8.9; CE Secure configuration

fs.protected_fifos is 1: a program that opens a file by name in a
world-writable sticky directory (/tmp), creating it if need be, is
refused when the name turns out to be a FIFO another user put there,
so it cannot be made to block on it or read what that user feeds it.
2 extends the rule to group-writable sticky directories, where a
member of the group can play the same trick. Every distribution ships
1 (systemd's 50-default.conf on Fedora and Arch, procps'
99-protect-links.conf on Debian and Ubuntu); the kernel's own default
is 0 (lin030w), which Alpine keeps. A hardening opportunity: Lynis
asks for 2, the CIS Benchmarks do not name the key.
  # sysctl -w fs.protected_fifos=2
